<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The ad-hoc counterpart of build/entitlements.mac.plist: what a local
  `npm run dist:mac` and the `macos-desktop` CI lane sign with, neither of which
  has a Developer ID.

  Identical to the Developer ID list plus disable-library-validation, and that
  one addition is the whole difference between the two files. Hardened runtime
  enables library validation, which admits only libraries signed by the loading
  binary's own Team ID (or by Apple). An ad-hoc signature carries NO Team ID, so
  the vendored `resources/node/bin/node` could not dlopen the ad-hoc-signed
  better-sqlite3 sitting beside it, and the failure would be a dlopen refusal at
  the first database query rather than anything visible at launch.
  electron-builder warns about exactly this pairing.

  This file is therefore the reason the test lane can exercise hardened runtime
  at all. What it verifies is real but partial: JIT, unsigned executable memory,
  and that the app still boots and serves under a hardened runtime. It cannot
  verify library validation, because it is the one thing it switches off. That
  half is only provable on a Developer ID build.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
